Talking Glove is an IoT-based communication system developed to assist individuals with speech impairments by converting hand gestures into spoken sentences. The project integrates an ESP32 microcontroller, a Node.js backend, and a React.js frontend to enable seamless real-time communication. The ESP32 detects predefined button combinations representing different gestures and transmits the corresponding gesture ID to the backend over a Wi-Fi connection. The backend processes the received data through RESTful APIs and maps each gesture to a predefined sentence. The React-based web application continuously retrieves the latest sentence from the backend, displays it on an interactive dashboard, and converts it into speech using the browser's Text-to-Speech functionality.

The application also includes a sentence management module that allows users to edit and customize predefined gesture-to-sentence mappings through a simple web interface without modifying the source code. An OLED display connected to the ESP32 provides real-time information such as Wi-Fi connection status, device IP address, and the currently detected gesture ID, making the system easier to monitor and debug.

This project demonstrates the integration of embedded systems, backend development, frontend development, REST API communication, and real-time networking into a single application. It showcases practical skills in IoT development, full-stack web development, API design, and user interface development while addressing a real-world accessibility problem.
